# Nielsen & Chuang, Theorem 10.6 — the normalizer `N(Gₙ)` is generated by H, S, CNOT

*(N&C §10.5.2, p. 461.)*

Any U with U Gn U-dag subset Gn is composable up to global phase from O(n^2) Hadamard/phase/CNOT
gates.

## Context

`Gₙ` is the `n`-qubit Pauli group (`Concrete.PauliGroup`, `iˢ ⊗ₖ σ_{aₖ}`), and the set of `U` with
`U Gₙ U† = Gₙ` is its **normalizer** `N(Gₙ)`; the Hadamard, phase (`S`), and controlled-NOT gates
are the **normalizer gates**. One direction — every product of these gates lies in `N(Gₙ)` — is the
Clifford-circuit conjugation backbone of Problem 10.3. Theorem 10.6 is the converse (with a gate
count): `N(Gₙ)` is *generated*, up to global phase, by H/S/CNOT, in `O(n²)` gates. N&C's proof is
the inductive synthesis of Exercise 10.40.

## Formalisation

The mathematics is about `2ⁿ×2ⁿ` unitary matrices (`Matrix.unitaryGroup`), the matrix Pauli group
(`Concrete.PauliGroup.toMat`), and Clifford circuits (`Concrete.CliffordCircuit.toMatrix`).
Compare Proposition 10.3 (`AxQM/Concrete/StabilizerIndependence.lean`) and Problem 10.3
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliString*.lean`). The formalisation is in `AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizer.lean`
(namespace `AxQM.Concrete`). It uses the Clifford-circuit and Pauli-group results of Problem 10.3
in `AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringCliffordCircuit.lean`, `AxQM/Concrete/PauliGroup.lean` and
`AxQM/Concrete/PauliString.lean`.

**Framework, easy direction, and the up-to-phase reduction:**

* `IsPauliNormalizer` — the normalizer predicate: `U` unitary with `U g U† ∈ Gₙ` for every
  `g ∈ Gₙ` (via the faithful representation `PauliGroup.toMat`). This is N&C's hypothesis.
* `exists_smul_one_of_commute_pauliString` — Schur's lemma for the Pauli group: a matrix commuting
  with every Pauli string is a scalar `c • 1` (the Pauli strings are a basis of the matrix algebra).
* `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_pauliString_eq` — **uniqueness up to a global phase**: two unitaries with
  the same conjugation action on the Pauli strings satisfy `U = c • V`, `‖c‖ = 1`. This is exactly
  the "up to a global phase" of the theorem — a normalizer unitary is fixed, up to a scalar, by its
  conjugation action — and it reduces Theorem 10.6 to realising that action by a Clifford circuit.
* `unitary_isGlobalPhase_of_conj_pauliString_eq_self` — Exercise 10.38 core: a unitary fixing every
  Pauli string under conjugation is a global phase `c • 1`.
* `cliffordCircuit_isPauliNormalizer` (with `CliffordCircuit.sign_eq_one_or_neg_one`,
  `CliffordCircuit.sign_eq_zmod4Pow`) — the **easy direction**: every Clifford circuit is a Pauli
  normalizer, i.e. `⟨H, S, CNOT⟩ ⊆ N(Gₙ)`.

**The normalizer's signed-permutation action, and the reduction to synthesis:**

* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_sign_conj_pauliString` — a normalizer conjugates each phase-free Pauli
  string to a `±1` multiple of a Pauli string (`U P_a U† = z • P_b`, `z ∈ {1, -1}`); the sign is
  real because `P_a` is an involution, so `(U P_a U†)² = 1` forces `z² = 1`.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_signedPerm` — the conjugation action bundled as a **`±1`-signed
  permutation** of the `4ⁿ` Pauli-string indices: a permutation `σ` and signs `ε ∈ {1, -1}` with
  `U P_a U† = ε a • P_{σ a}`. This is the combinatorial datum Exercise 10.40 synthesises.
* `unitary_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit_of_conj_eq` — the **reduction of the converse to synthesis**: a
  unitary matching a Clifford circuit's signed permutation equals it up to a global phase, so the
  converse becomes "synthesise a Clifford circuit realising the normalizer's signed permutation".

**Synthesis scaffolding — circuit composition + the single-qubit `X,Z` reduction:**

* `CliffordCircuit.toMatrix_append` / `act_append` / `sign_append`
  (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringCliffordCircuit.lean`) — how the matrix, phase-free action and `±1`
  phase of a Clifford circuit compose across a gate-list append `U ++ V` (`V` first): the algebra by
  which a synthesised circuit is glued from smaller pieces (used by Exercise 10.40's induction).
* `pauliString_two_eq_smul_mul` — the single-qubit identity `Y = i · X Z`
  (`P_{fun _ => 2} = i • (P_X · P_Z)`), expressing the third single-qubit Pauli through the two
  generators `X, Z`.
* `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_singleQubit_XZ` — **a single-qubit unitary is determined, up to a global
  phase, by how it conjugates `X` and `Z`.** Two one-qubit unitaries agreeing on `U X U†` and
  `U Z U†` agree up to a unit scalar: `I ↦ I` by unitarity and `Y ↦ …` by multiplicativity of
  conjugation, so the two generators fix the whole action. This is the single-qubit specialisation
  of `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_pauliString_eq`, and it reduces the base case (Ex 10.40 part 1) from
  *matching the full signed permutation* to *matching the images of `X` and `Z`* — the two targets
  an `H/S`-word synthesis must hit.

**The single-qubit base case (N&C Exercise 10.40 part 1):**

* `conj_pauliString_index_ne_zero` — a unitary conjugates a non-identity Pauli string to a
  non-identity one (`U P_a U† = z • P_b`, `z ≠ 0`, `a ≠ 0` ⟹ `b ≠ 0`), by trace-preservation; this
  is why a normalizer sends `X` and `Z` to genuine (non-identity) Pauli strings.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_singleQubit_act` — `H` and `S` realise every permutation of the
  single-qubit Pauli indices `{X, Y, Z}` (their actions are the transpositions `X↔Z`, `X↔Y`, which
  generate `S₃`), so their words hit any distinct non-identity `(X, Z)` image pair.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_singleQubit_signFix` — every `±1` sign pattern on `X, Z` is realised by a
  trivial-action Pauli-conjugation word (`Z = S²`, `X = H S² H`, `Y`-type composite).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_singleQubit_act_sign` — **single-qubit synthesis.** Gluing a permutation
  word to a sign-fix word (`act_append` / `sign_append`) yields, for every valid signed permutation
  (distinct non-identity targets, `±1` signs — the 24 elements of the single-qubit Clifford group
  mod phase), a Clifford circuit realising it exactly on `X` and `Z`.
* `isPauliNormalizer_singleQubit_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit` — **Theorem 10.6 for `n = 1`.** Every
  single-qubit Pauli normalizer `U` equals a global phase times an `H/S`-word: `U = c • C.toMatrix`,
  `‖c‖ = 1`. Its `X, Z` action (a signed permutation fixing `I`) is synthesised by a Clifford
  circuit `C`, and `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_singleQubit_XZ` upgrades the match to a global phase.

**A normalizer's action is symplectic — the invariant driving the multi-qubit
induction:**

* `unitaryConj_commute_iff` — **conjugation by a unitary preserves commutation.** For unitary `U`,
  `Commute (U P U†) (U Q U†) ↔ Commute P Q`: conjugation `M ↦ U M U†` is the `*`-algebra
  automorphism `Unitary.conjStarAlgAut ℂ _ ⟨U, hU⟩`, and commutation transports along it both ways
  (`Commute.map`, via `f.symm` for the reverse). A general matrix fact, reused wherever a
  normalizer's action must respect commutation.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_signedPerm_symplectic` — **a normalizer's signed permutation is
  symplectic.** Strengthening `exists_signedPerm`: the induced permutation `σ` of the Pauli-string
  indices preserves the binary symplectic form (`pauliAnticommCount`, mod 2) of Exercise 10.33 —
  `Even (pauliAnticommCount (σ a) (σ b)) ↔ Even (pauliAnticommCount a b)`. From
  `unitaryConj_commute_iff` (the ±1 signs cancel) and the criterion `pauliString_commute_iff`. This
  is the structural invariant the inductive synthesis of Exercise 10.40 parts 2–3 rests on: a
  normalizer maps anticommuting Pauli strings to anticommuting ones — precisely the fact N&C open
  part 2 with ("since `Z₁` and `X₁` anticommute, `U Z₁ U†` and `U X₁ U†` anticommute").

**Wire relabeling — embedding a Clifford circuit along an injection of wires:** the
transport (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliStringCliffordCircuit.lean`) used in the multi-qubit induction.

* `CliffordGate.relabel` / `CliffordCircuit.relabel` — relabel a Clifford circuit on `m` wires onto
  `n` wires along an embedding `f : Fin m ↪ Fin n` (`had j ↦ had (f j)`, `cnot c t ↦ cnot (f c)
  (f t)`; wire distinctness preserved by injectivity).
* `CliffordCircuit.act_relabel_comp` / `act_relabel_embed` — the relabeled circuit's **phase-free
  action pulls back along `f`**: on the embedded wires, `(relabel f C).act g (f i) = C.act (g ∘ f)
  i`.
* `CliffordCircuit.sign_relabel` — its **`±1` phase pulls back** too: `(relabel f C).sign g =
  C.sign (g ∘ f)`.
* `CliffordCircuit.act_relabel_of_forall_ne` — a relabeled circuit **leaves every wire outside the
  range of `f` untouched**.
  Together these let the inductive synthesis embed the `n`-qubit inductive hypothesis into a
  sub-register of the `(n+1)`-qubit register (choosing `f` to pick out those wires) — and run the
  single-qubit base case on any chosen wire (`f = fun _ => j`) — realising on the embedded wires
  exactly the signed permutation of the sub-register circuit, and disturbing nothing else.

**Generator-determinacy — the reduction of multi-qubit synthesis to matching on the `2n`
generators:** used in parts 2 and 3 of Exercise 10.40
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGenerators.lean`).

* `pauliString_peel_wire` — **peel one wire off a Pauli string**: `P_a = P_{a'} · P_{b}` with
  `a' = Function.update a i 0` (wire `i` zeroed) and `b = Function.update 0 i (a i)` (the
  single-wire Pauli on wire `i`). The two factors are supported on disjoint wires, so the product
  law contributes index `a` and phase `1` — the recursion expressing any Pauli string as a product
  of single-wire generators.
* `pauliString_yGenPauli_eq_smul_mul` — **`Yᵢ = i · Xᵢ Zᵢ` on any wire**, the multi-qubit analogue
  of `pauliString_two_eq_smul_mul`: the third single-wire Pauli through the two generators `Xᵢ, Zᵢ`.
* `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_generators` — **generator-determinacy.** Two unitaries `U, V` conjugating
  every `Xᵢ` and `Zᵢ` identically satisfy `U = c • V`, `‖c‖ = 1`. The multi-qubit generalisation of
  the single-qubit `unitary_eq_smul_of_conj_singleQubit_XZ`: agreement propagates from the
  `2n` generators to every Pauli string, by multiplicativity of conjugation
  (`conj_mul_conj_of_conjTranspose_mul_self`) and a `Finset.induction` on the support that peels the
  string wire by wire (`pauliString_peel_wire`) down to `Xᵢ`, `Zᵢ`, `Yᵢ = i Xᵢ Zᵢ` and the identity.
* `unitary_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit_of_conj_generators` — **the converse reduces to matching on
  generators.** If `U` conjugates every `Xᵢ` and `Zᵢ` exactly as a Clifford circuit `C` does, then
  `U = c • C.toMatrix`, `‖c‖ = 1`. The multi-qubit refinement of
  `unitary_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit_of_conj_eq` (which matched on *all* Pauli strings): the inductive
  synthesis of Exercise 10.40 parts 2–3 need only reproduce the normalizer's action on the `2n`
  generators `Xᵢ, Zᵢ`, not on all `4ⁿ` Pauli strings.

**Single-wire Clifford synthesis on any wire — the per-wire atom of the sweep**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerSweep.lean`).

* `wireEmbedding` — the wire-selecting embedding `Fin 1 ↪ Fin n` picking out one wire `j`.
* `CliffordCircuit.onWire j C₀` — run a single-wire circuit `C₀` on wire `j` of the `n`-wire
  register (`relabel (wireEmbedding j) C₀`), with the full action/phase characterisation
  `onWire_act` (wire `j` transforms by `C₀`'s single-wire action), `onWire_act_of_ne` (every wire
  `k ≠ j` untouched), `onWire_sign` (phase is `C₀`'s at wire `j`'s letter), and `onWire_act_update`
  (on a single-wire Pauli `update 0 j c`, the image is `update 0 j (C₀.act c)`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_onWire_act_sign` — **single-wire signed-permutation synthesis on an
  arbitrary wire.** For any wire `j`, distinct non-identity target letters `aX, aZ` and signs
  `zX, zZ ∈ {±1}`, an `n`-wire Clifford circuit realising, on wire `j`, `Xⱼ ↦ zX·(aX on j)` and
  `Zⱼ ↦ zZ·(aZ on j)` exactly, while fixing every other wire. The multi-qubit lift of
  `exists_cliffordCircuit_singleQubit_act_sign` (the base case), and the per-wire building block the
  Exercise 10.40 sweep runs on each wire in turn.

**The single-qubit layer — rotate every wire's Pauli letter at once**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerLayer.lean`): the sweep's first move — one (independently chosen)
single-wire Clifford applied to *every* wire simultaneously, the parallel companion of the
single-wire `onWire`.

* `CliffordCircuit.singleQubitLayer` — apply the single-wire circuit `f i` to wire `i` for every
  wire `i` (`layerOn f (List.finRange n)`, folding the per-wire `onWire i (f i)` circuits), with the
  clean characterisations `singleQubitLayer_act` (the layer acts **wire-by-wire independently**:
  `(singleQubitLayer f).act g i = (f i).act (fun _ => g i) 0`) and `singleQubitLayer_sign` (its `±1`
  phase is the **product of the per-wire phases**: `(singleQubitLayer f).sign g = ∏ i, (f i).sign
  (fun _ => g i)`). The per-wire independence is exactly what lets the induction diagonalise one
  generator's image while reading off the layer's effect on another's.
* `CliffordCircuit.rotateToX` / `exists_singleQubitLayer_act_diagX` — **step 1 of the tableau
  sweep**: a single-qubit layer rotating any Pauli string `a` to its all-`X`-on-support form
  (`act (singleQubitLayer f) a k = if a k = 0 then 0 else 1`), returned as the per-wire family so
  the same layer's action on any other string stays available via `singleQubitLayer_act`.

**CNOT clearing — collapse the all-`X` image to a single generator (sweep step 2)**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerCnotClear.lean`): the second move of the Exercise 10.40 tableau
sweep, run after the layer's diagonalisation, and the *inverse reading* of the Problem 10.3 CNOT
`X`-spread.

* `act_cnotFan_collapse` — **a fan of CNOTs collapses an all-`X` string to its pivot.** For a string
  carrying `X` on wire `c` and on every wire of a distinct list `ts` (all in the support), the fan
  `cnotFan c ts` (control `c`, targets `ts`) sends it to `X` on `c`, `I` on each target, and every
  other wire unchanged (`act (cnotFan c ts) a k = if k = c then 1 else if k ∈ ts then 0 else a k`).
  A single `CNOT_{c→t}` on an `(X_c, X_t)` pair sends `X_c X_t ↦ X_c` (`x_t += x_c`, Fig 10.7),
  so the control keeps its `X` (`cnotCtrlPauli 1 1 = 1`) while each target's `X` is cleared
  (`cnotTgtPauli 1 1 = 0`); an induction on `ts` over `act_cnotFan_cons_of_ne`.
* `sign_cnotStep_of_ne` / `sign_cnotFan_collapse` — **the clearing introduces no sign**
  (`sign (cnotFan c ts) a = 1`): every step's CNOT tableau sign is `cnotPauliSign 1 1 = 1`.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleX` — **sweep step 2.** For any all-`X` string `a` with a
  pivot `c` in its support (`a c = 1`), a Clifford circuit realises `a ↦ xGenPauli c` exactly with
  phase `1` (targets = the rest of the support, as a `Finset.filter`). This reduces a diagonalised
  generator image `U X₁ U†` to the standard `X₁`.

**The `Z`-generator sweep companion — diagonalise to all-`Z` and collapse to a single
`Z`** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerZClear.lean`): the `Z`-basis mirror of the two preceding
`X`-generator moves, reducing `U Z₁ U†` the way they reduce `U X₁ U†`.

* `CliffordCircuit.rotateToZ` / `rotateToZ_act` — a single-wire Clifford rotating a Pauli letter to
  `Z` (fixing `I`): `H` for `X` (`hadamardPauli 1 = 3`), `H S` for `Y` (`S` first, `phasePauli 2 =
  1`; then `H`), and nothing for `Z`/`I`.
* `exists_singleQubitLayer_act_diagZ` — **sweep step 1 (`Z` basis).** A single-qubit layer sends any
  Pauli string `a` to its all-`Z`-on-support form (`act (singleQubitLayer f) a k = if a k = 0 then 0
  else 3`), returned as the per-wire family `f = fun k => rotateToZ (a k)` so the same layer's
  action on any *other* string stays available via `singleQubitLayer_act` (needed to track
  `U X₁ U†` while diagonalising `U Z₁ U†`). The mirror of `exists_singleQubitLayer_act_diagX`.
* `cnotFanInto` (with `act_cnotFanInto_cons_of_ne`, `act_cnotFanInto_collapse`,
  `sign_cnotFanInto_collapse`) — the **reverse CNOT fan** `CNOT_{t→c}` for each `t ∈ ts` (targets
  folded into one collector wire `c`), the control/target mirror of `cnotFan`. On an all-`Z` string
  with pivot `c` it clears the `Z` off every control (`cnotCtrlPauli 3 3 = 0`) while keeping `Z` on
  the target `c` (`cnotTgtPauli 3 3 = 3`), with trivial phase (`cnotPauliSign 3 3 = 1`) — the
  `Z`-basis mirror of the `X`-clearing fan (`z_c += z_t` rather than `x_t += x_c`, Figure 10.7).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleZ` — **sweep step 2 (`Z` basis).** For
  any all-`Z` string `a` with a pivot `c` in its support (`a c = 3`), a Clifford circuit realises
  `a ↦ zGenPauli c` exactly with phase `1` (controls = the rest of the support, as a
  `Finset.filter`). The mirror of `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleX`: it reduces a
  diagonalised second-generator image `U Z₁ U†` to the standard `Z₁`.

**Exact single-generator reduction — collapse a Pauli string to a standard
`X_c`/`Z_c` with the phase pinned to `+1`** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerReduceGenerator.lean`):
assembles the
diagonalise and CNOT-clear sweep moves into a *single-step, exact*
reduction of one generator image — the first move of the Exercise 10.40 first-qubit reduction.

* `exists_cliffordCircuit_xGen_signFlip` / `exists_cliffordCircuit_zGen_signFlip` — **sign-flip
  gadgets**: a Clifford circuit fixing a generator's index while negating its phase
  (`act C (xGenPauli c) = xGenPauli c`, `sign C (xGenPauli c) = -1`, and the `Z` mirror). Each is
  the single-wire `onWire` synthesis on wire `c` (`X ↦ -X, Z ↦ +Z`, i.e. a `Z_c`
  conjugation; and the mirror). These correct the residual `±1` phase of the assembled reduction.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceToXGen` — **exact reduction to `X_c`.** For any Pauli string `a` and
  any pivot `c` in its support (`a c ≠ 0`), a Clifford circuit `C` with `act C a = xGenPauli c` and
  `sign C a = 1`. Diagonalise `a` to all-`X` (the pivot survives, `a c ≠ 0`), CNOT-clear to `X_c`
  (phase-free), then read off the composite's `±1` phase (`CliffordCircuit.sign_eq_one_or_neg_one`)
  and, if `-1`, prepend the `X`-sign-flip gadget. Reduces a normalizer's generator image `U X₁ U†`
  to the standard `X_c`.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceToZGen` — the **`Z`-basis mirror** (`act C a = zGenPauli c`,
  `sign C a = 1`), reducing the second generator image `U Z₁ U†` to the standard `Z_c`.

**The Clifford action is symplectic, and the symplectic first move of the pair
reduction** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerSymplectic.lean`): the structural invariant carrying the
first-qubit reduction from *one* generator image to the *anticommuting pair* — precisely how
N&C open Exercise 10.40 part 2 ("since `Z₁` and `X₁` anticommute, `U Z₁ U†` and `U X₁ U†`
anticommute").

* `CliffordCircuit.act_pauliAnticommCount_even_iff` — **the Clifford action is symplectic**: a
  circuit's phase-free action preserves the mod-2 symplectic form of Exercise 10.33,
  `Even (pauliAnticommCount (act C g) (act C h)) ↔ Even (pauliAnticommCount g h)`. The circuit-level
  counterpart of the normalizer-level `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_signedPerm_symplectic`,
  proved the same way (`act C` = conjugation by the unitary `toMatrix C` via `conj_pauliString`;
  `unitaryConj_commute_iff` cancels the `±1` phases; `pauliString_commute_iff` gives the parity).
* `pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli` / `…_zGenPauli` — **single-generator positions**: the symplectic
  pairing against a standard generator is one check bit, `pauliAnticommCount h (xGenPauli c) =
  pauliZBit (h c)` and `pauliAnticommCount h (zGenPauli c) = pauliXBit (h c)`; with the parity
  readings `odd_pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli_iff` (`h` anticommutes with `X_c` iff `h c ∈ {Y, Z}`)
  and `odd_pauliAnticommCount_zGenPauli_iff` (with `Z_c` iff `h c ∈ {X, Y}`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceFirstOfPair` — **the symplectic first move of the pair reduction.**
  For anticommuting `p, q` (`Odd (pauliAnticommCount p q)`), a Clifford circuit `C` and pivot wire
  `c` with `act C p = xGenPauli c`, `sign C p = 1`, and the partner image `act C q` *still
  anticommuting with `X_c`* (`act C q c = 2 ∨ act C q c = 3`). Assembles the exact reduction of `p`
  with the symplectic invariant: `p` (an anticommuting partner) is non-identity, so it reduces to
  `X_c`; the action preserves anticommutation, so `act C q` keeps a `Z` factor on the shared pivot
  `c`. The pair reduction finishes this configuration by reducing `act C q` to `Z_c` while fixing
  `X_c`, bringing the pair to the standard `(X_c, Z_c)`.

**The first-qubit reduction — an anticommuting pair to standard `(X_c, Z_c)`**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerPairReduce.lean`): completes the first-qubit reduction begun above,
carrying an anticommuting generator pair to the canonical `(X_c, Z_c)` on a common pivot wire.

* `CliffordCircuit.rotateToZFixingX` (with `_act_self`, `_act_one`) /
  `CliffordCircuit.rotateToZ_act_zero`
  — the single-wire atoms of the **`X_c`-preserving diagonalisation**: a pivot gate sending `q_c ∈
  {Y, Z}` to `Z` while fixing `X` (identity for `Z`; `H S H`, which fixes `X` and swaps `Y ↔ Z`, for
  `Y`), and the fact that every off-pivot `rotateToZ` gate fixes the identity letter.
* `act_cnotFanInto_xGenPauli` — the **reverse CNOT fan fixes `X_c`** (`c ∉ ts`): every `CNOT_{t→c}`
  targets the pivot `c`, and an `X` on a CNOT *target* is preserved (`cnotTgtPauli 0 1 = 1`,
  `cnotCtrlPauli 0 1 = 0`); only an `X` on the *control* spreads. An induction on `ts`.
* `exists_cnotFanInto_toSingleZ_fixingX` — the CNOT-clear collapsing an all-`Z` string to `Z_c`
  *while fixing `X_c`* (the `X_c`-preserving strengthening of
  `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleZ`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceSecondFixingX` — **the `X_c`-preserving `Z`-reduction.** For a Pauli
  string `q` with `q c ∈ {Y, Z}`, a Clifford circuit `D` with `act D q = zGenPauli c` and
  `act D (xGenPauli c) = xGenPauli c` (index level only). A custom diagonalising layer (trivial on
  the pivot's `X`, `rotateToZ` elsewhere) sends `q` to all-`Z` while fixing `X_c`; the reverse CNOT
  fan collapses it to `Z_c` while again fixing `X_c`. Unlike the generic `reduceToZGen`,
  whose layer and `Z_c`-conjugation sign-flip both disturb `X_c`.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_fixPairSigns` — **sign normalisation of a reduced pair.**
  Given any Clifford
  circuit already sending `p ↦ X_c`, `q ↦ Z_c` on the index level, two disjoint `onWire` gadgets pin
  the two independent residual `±1` phases to `+1` (one flips `X_c`'s sign fixing `Z_c`, the
  other flips `Z_c`'s sign fixing `X_c`). It is used per pair in the `n+1` induction
  (`isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reducePair` — **the first-qubit reduction.** For an
  anticommuting pair `p, q` (`Odd (pauliAnticommCount p q)`), a Clifford circuit `E` and pivot wire
  `c` reducing the pair to the canonical generators *exactly*: `act E p = xGenPauli c`, `sign E p =
  1`, `act E q = zGenPauli c`, `sign E q = 1`. A three-move composition:
  the symplectic first move `C`
  (`p ↦ X_c`, `q` still `Y`/`Z` on `c`), the `X_c`-preserving `Z`-reduction `D` of `act C q`,
  and `exists_cliffordCircuit_fixPairSigns` on `D ++ C`. This is the base of the
  multi-qubit induction
  (Figure 10.9): a normalizer's first anticommuting generator pair is brought to standard `(X_c,
  Z_c)`.

**The wire-swap Clifford circuit — transpose two wires' Pauli letters**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerSwap.lean`): the "swap the pivot to a designated first wire" move of
Figure 10.9, built from the standard three-`CNOT` swap identity.

* `swapWires a b` — the wire-swap circuit `[CNOT_{a→b}, CNOT_{b→a}, CNOT_{a→b}]` (empty for
  `a = b`), the Clifford realisation of `SWAP_{a,b}`.
* `act_swapWires` / `act_swapWires_eq` — **the swap transposes the two wires' letters**:
  `(swapWires a b).act g k = g (Equiv.swap a b k)`. On the swapped wires this is the composition of
  the three `CNOT` tableau steps (`cnotPauliString`), a finite `Fin 4 × Fin 4` `decide`; off them
  every gate leaves the wire fixed (`cnotPauliString_of_ne`).
* `sign_swapWires` — **the swap acquires no phase**: `(swapWires a b).sign g = 1`, since `SWAP` only
  permutes tensor factors — the three `CNOT` tableau signs multiply to `+1` on every letter pair.
* `act_swapWires_xGenPauli` / `act_swapWires_zGenPauli` — **the swap moves a standard generator
  between the two wires** (`(swapWires a b).act (xGenPauli a) = xGenPauli b`, and the `Z` mirror) —
  the exact use the induction makes of it, relocating a reduced pivot `(X_c, Z_c)` to a chosen wire.

**The first-wire reduction — bring a normalizer to fix `X₀`, `Z₀` on wire 0**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerFirstPair.lean`): assembles the pair reduction and the wire swap into
the opening move of Figure 10.9
— left-composing a normalizer `U` with a Clifford circuit so the composite fixes the first-wire
generators, the reduction that peels the first wire off the `n+1`-qubit induction.

* `IsPauliNormalizer.mul` — **the normalizer `N(Gₙ)` is closed under matrix multiplication**: the
  product of two normalizers is unitary (submonoid closure) and conjugation composes
  (`(A B) p (A B)† = A (B p B†) A† ∈ Gₙ`). This is what makes the composite `B.toMatrix * U` again a
  normalizer, so the induction can recurse on it.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_cliffordCircuit_fixFirstPair` — **the first-wire reduction.** For any
  `(n+1)`-qubit normalizer `U`, a Clifford circuit `B` such that `B.toMatrix * U` is again a
  normalizer and **fixes the first-wire generators exactly** under conjugation:
  `(B U) P_{X₀} (B U)† = P_{X₀}` and `(B U) P_{Z₀} (B U)† = P_{Z₀}`. The images `σ X₀`, `σ Z₀` of
  the first-wire generators under the signed permutation anticommute (`X₀, Z₀` anticommute
  and `σ` is symplectic), so `reducePair` brings the pair to `(X_c, Z_c)` on a pivot
  wire `c`, `swapWires c 0` relocates the pivot to wire 0, and a single-wire sign gadget on
  wire 0 cancels the two residual `±1` phases `ε(X₀), ε(Z₀)` (each squaring to `1`). This is
  the first move of the multi-qubit induction: after it, the remaining generators are forced onto
  the last `n` wires and the inductive hypothesis applies.

**Pivot-wire decoupling — a normalizer fixing `X₀, Z₀` sends every other generator to a Pauli on the
last `n` wires** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerDecouple.lean`): the second move of the Figure 10.9
inductive step, consuming the first-wire reduction.

* `pauliString_index_eq_zero_of_commute_gen` — **only the identity commutes with both `X_c` and
  `Z_c`.** A Pauli string `b` commuting with both standard generators `X_c` and `Z_c` has the
  identity letter on wire `c` (`b c = 0`): the symplectic criterion (Exercise 10.33,
  `pauliString_commute_iff`) forces `b c ∉ {Y, Z}` (from `X_c`) and `b c ∉ {X, Y}` (from `Z_c`),
  leaving `b c = I`.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.conj_apply_zero_eq_zero` — **wire-`0` decoupling.** A Pauli normalizer `M`
  fixing `X₀` and `Z₀` under conjugation sends every Pauli string trivial on wire `0` (`a 0 = 0`) to
  a signed Pauli `z • P_b` (`z = ±1`) again trivial on wire `0` (`b 0 = 0`). Since `a` is `I` on
  wire `0` it commutes with `X₀, Z₀`; conjugation preserves commutation (`unitaryConj_commute_iff`)
  and `M` fixes `X₀, Z₀`, so `z • P_b` commutes with both; stripping the nonzero sign, `P_b` does
  too, whence `b 0 = 0`. Applied to the generators `Xᵢ, Zᵢ` (`i ≠ 0`, each `I` on wire `0`), this
  forces their images onto the last `n` wires — the decoupling that lets the multi-qubit induction
  restrict `M` to an `n`-qubit normalizer on the sub-register `{1, …, n}`.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_conj_xGenPauli_supportedOffZero` /
  `…_exists_conj_zGenPauli_supportedOffZero` — **the generator specialisation** of
  `conj_apply_zero_eq_zero`: for `i ≠ 0`, `M Xᵢ M†` and `M Zᵢ M†` are `±1` multiples of Pauli
  strings supported on the last `n` wires (`b 0 = 0`) — the exact form the inductive step consumes
  as an `n`-qubit normalizer's generator action.

**The Clifford-circuit inverse — `(inverse C).toMatrix = C.toMatrixᴴ`**
(`AxQM/Concrete/CliffordCircuitInverse.lean`): used by the multi-qubit induction to
re-express a reduced normalizer as a global phase times a Clifford circuit. Self-contained
(independent of the first-wire reduction and the decoupling).

* `kronWireGate_mul` / `kronWireGate_conjTranspose` — two general single-wire facts: placing `G · H`
  on a wire equals placing `G` then `H` (`kronFamily_mul`), and the adjoint passes through
  (`(kronWireGate j G)ᴴ = kronWireGate j Gᴴ`, from `kronFamily_conjTranspose`).
* `sMatrix_mul_self_mul_self_eq_conjTranspose` — the phase gate has order `4`, so `S³ = Sᴴ`
  (`S = diag(1, i)`, both sides `diag(1, -i)`); this is what makes `[S, S, S]` an inverse word for
  `S` (`S⁻¹ = S³`).
* `CliffordGate.invList` (with `CliffordCircuit.toMatrix_invList`) — the **inverse word** of an
  elementary gate: `[H]` for `H`, `[CNOT]` for `CNOT` (self-adjoint involutions), `[S, S, S]` for
  `S`; with `(invList γ).toMatrix = γ.toMatrixᴴ`.
* `CliffordCircuit.inverse` (with `toMatrix_inverse`) — the **inverse of a circuit**: reverse the
  gate list and replace each gate by its inverse word, with `(inverse C).toMatrix = C.toMatrixᴴ`.
  By induction on the gate list (`toMatrix_append`, `toMatrix_invList`, `conjTranspose_mul` —
  whose product-reversal matches the list reversal). Because a Clifford circuit is unitary, this is
  a genuine two-sided inverse (`toMatrix_mul_inverse`, `toMatrix_inverse_mul`). This is what lets
  the Figure 10.9 induction turn `U = Bᴴ • (phase • B'.toMatrix)` into a global phase times the
  Clifford circuit `inverse B ++ B'`.

**The wire-`0` tensor restriction — `packaging` step, tensor decomposition + unitarity**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerRestrict.lean`): the first half of the Figure 10.9 inductive step's
*packaging* of `M`'s decoupled action as a bona fide `n`-qubit operator, consuming the first-wire
reduction and preparing the recursion.

* `embedLast` — the **block-scalar embedding** `I₂ ⊗ X` of an `n`-qubit matrix `X` onto the last
  `n` of `n+1` wires (identity on wire `0`), with its algebra `embedLast_mul`, `embedLast_one`,
  `embedLast_conjTranspose`, `embedLast_injective`; the tensor-with-identity functor whose image is
  exactly the operators fixing wire `0`.
* `firstWireRestrict` — the **restriction** `M' a b := M (0 ∷ a) (0 ∷ b)` of an `(n+1)`-qubit
  matrix to the wire-`0`-trivial block, the `M'` of a factorisation `M = I₂ ⊗ M'`.
* `pauliString_zGenPauli_zero_eq_diagonal` / `pauliString_xGenPauli_zero_apply` — the sparse entry
  structure of `Z₀` (diagonal, `±1` by the wire-`0` bit) and `X₀` (the wire-`0` bit-flip
  permutation matrix), the two facts the factorisation reads off.
* `eq_embedLast_firstWireRestrict` — **the tensor decomposition `M = I₂ ⊗ M'`.** A unitary `M`
  fixing `X₀, Z₀` under conjugation equals `embedLast (firstWireRestrict M)`. Fixing `X₀, Z₀` up to
  conjugation is, for a unitary, commuting with them: commuting with the diagonal `Z₀` kills every
  entry mixing the two wire-`0` sectors (`i₀ ≠ j₀`); commuting with the bit-flip `X₀` identifies the
  two diagonal sectors — the commutant fact that `⟨X, Z⟩` on wire `0` is all of `M₂(ℂ)`, whose
  commutant in `M_{2ⁿ⁺¹}(ℂ)` is `I₂ ⊗ M_{2ⁿ}(ℂ)`. Only unitarity is used (a normalizer supplies it
  through `.1`), so it is stated over `Matrix.unitaryGroup`.
* `firstWireRestrict_mem_unitaryGroup` — **`M'` is unitary**, read off `M = I₂ ⊗ M'` from
  `Mᴴ M = 1` (its wire-`0`-trivial block collapses to `M'ᴴ M' = 1`), so it is a bona fide `n`-qubit
  operator the inductive hypothesis can consume.

**The wire-`0` restriction is an `n`-qubit normalizer — the second half of the
`packaging`** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerRestrictNormalizer.lean`): the restriction by which
the induction restricts `M` to the sub-register `{1, …, n}` and recurse.

* `pauliString_cons_zero` / `embedLast_smul` — `P_{cons 0 a} = I₂ ⊗ P_a` and the linearity of
  `I₂ ⊗ ·` in the scalar.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.conj_pauliString_cons` — the **block-diagonal intertwining**: for a normalizer
  `M` fixing `X₀, Z₀`, `M · P_{cons 0 a} · M† = I₂ ⊗ (M' · P_a · M'†)` (`M' = firstWireRestrict M`),
  from `M = I₂ ⊗ M'` and multiplicativity of `I₂ ⊗ ·`. `M`'s action on the last-`n`-wire Pauli
  strings is exactly `M'`'s, wrapped in `I₂ ⊗ ·`.
* `firstWireRestrict_conj_pauliString` / `firstWireRestrict_isPauliNormalizer` — the `±1` signed
  action of `M'` and, from it, **the restriction is a normalizer**: `firstWireRestrict M` is an
  `n`-qubit Pauli normalizer (unitary as above; group condition by rebuilding the
  fourth-root-of-unity phase).

**The multi-qubit inductive synthesis (N&C Exercise 10.40 parts 2–3)**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerInduction.lean`): assembles the whole machinery into the *existence*
content of the converse — every normalizer is a global phase times a Clifford circuit.

* `CliffordCircuit.act_const_zero` / `sign_const_zero` — a Clifford circuit fixes the identity Pauli
  string (`act C 0 = 0`, `sign C 0 = 1`).
* `xGenPauli_zero_eq_cons` / `zGenPauli_zero_eq_cons` / `xGenPauli_succ_eq_cons` /
  `zGenPauli_succ_eq_cons` — the standard generators as `Fin.cons` strings (`X₀ = cons 1 0`,
  `Xᵢ₊₁ = cons 0 Xᵢ`, and the `Z` mirrors), exposing the wire-`0` head.
* `CliffordCircuit.relabelSucc_act_cons` / `relabelSucc_sign_cons` — the action and `±1` phase of a
  circuit relabeled onto the last `n` wires, on a `Fin.cons` string: wire `0` untouched, the last
  `n` wires transform by the circuit on the tail.
* `smul_conjTranspose_conj_cancel` — a unit global phase cancels in a conjugation.
* `isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit` — every `n`-qubit Pauli
  normalizer `U` equals a global phase times a Clifford circuit, `U = c • C.toMatrix`, `‖c‖ = 1`.
  Induction on
  `n` (Figure 10.9): the base `n = 0` is a `1×1` unitary; the step reduces `U` to `M = B U` fixing
  `X₀, Z₀`, restricts to the `n`-qubit normalizer `M'` (`firstWireRestrict`), recurses (IH) to a
  Clifford `C'`, embeds it onto the last `n` wires, matches `M` on every generator
  (generator-determinacy), and re-expresses `U` via the Clifford-circuit inverse.

**The gate-count length foundation** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCount.lean`):
the reusable length arithmetic of the circuit-building blocks, used to thread the `O(n²)` bound
through the Figure 10.9 synthesis. A `CliffordCircuit n` is a
`List (CliffordGate n)`, so its **gate count is its `List.length`**.

* `CliffordCircuit.length_relabel` / `length_onWire` — wire relabeling (a `List.map`) preserves the
  gate count.
* `CliffordCircuit.length_layerOn_le` / `length_singleQubitLayer_le` — a single-qubit layer costs
  `≤ n · m₀` gates when each per-wire word has `≤ m₀` gates (one bounded word on each of `n` wires);
  with `length_rotateToX_le` (`≤ 1`), `length_rotateToZ_le` (`≤ 2`), `length_rotateToZFixingX_le`
  (`≤ 3`) supplying the constant `m₀` for the sweep's rotation layers.
* `length_cnotStep_le` (`≤ 1`), `length_cnotFan_le` / `length_cnotFanInto_le` (`≤ |ts|`),
  `length_swapWires_le` (`≤ 3`) — a CNOT fan costs one gate per target; a wire swap is `≤ 3` gates.
* `CliffordGate.length_invList_le` (`≤ 3`) / `CliffordCircuit.length_inverse_le`
  (`≤ 3 · |C|`) — inverting a circuit at most triples the gate count (`S⁻¹ = S³` the worst word).
* `CliffordCircuit.length_inverse_append_relabel_le` — the per-level overhead of the Figure 10.9
  recurrence `C_{n+1} = inverse B ++ relabel C'`: `≤ 3 · |B| + |C'|` gates. With `|B| ≤ O(n)` (shown
  below) this unrolls to the `O(n²)` total.

**The single-generator reduction gate-count bound — first threading step of the
per-level linear bound** (`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCountReduce.lean`): length-annotated
**companions** `…_length_le` of the reduction sweep, threading the length calculus up through the
generator reduction. They re-exhibit each circuit with a `List.length` bound, beside the original
`∃`-only lemmas.

* `exists_singleQubitLayer_act_diagX_length_le` (`≤ n`) / `…diagZ_length_le` (`≤ 2·n`) — the
  diagonalising single-qubit layers cost one bounded rotation word (`rotateToX ≤ 1`,
  `rotateToZ ≤ 2`) on each of the `n` wires (`length_singleQubitLayer_le`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleX_length_le` / `…toSingleZ_length_le` (each `≤ n`) —
  the CNOT-clearing fans cost at most one CNOT per support wire
  (`length_cnotFan_le`/`…FanInto_le`), and the support is a `Finset.filter` of `Fin n`, so `≤ n`.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_xGen_signFlip_length_le` (`≤ 2`, `onWire c [S, S]`) /
  `…zGen_signFlip_length_le` (`≤ 4`, `onWire c [H, S, S, H]`) — the residual-phase gadgets as
  explicit single-wire Pauli conjugations (`Z = S²`, `X = H S² H`), constant-length.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceToXGen_length_le` (`≤ 2·n + 2`) / `…reduceToZGen_length_le`
  (`≤ 3·n + 4`) — the exact single-generator reduction
  (`layer ++ cnotClear ++ signGadget`) costs `O(n)` gates, the per-generator atom of `|B| ≤ O(n)`.
  The **constant** base-gadget bounds are in
  `AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCountGadgets.lean`
  (`exists_cliffordCircuit_onWire_act_sign_length_le`, `≤ 9` gates, and the single-qubit synthesis
  it wraps).

**The pair-reduction gate-count bound — the anticommuting-pair reduction costs `O(n)` gates**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCountPair.lean`): threads the length calculus (above) up through
the first-qubit reduction. Length-annotated companions `…_length_le` of the pair-reduction sweep.

* `exists_cnotFanInto_toSingleZ_fixingX_length_le` (`≤ n`) — the reverse CNOT fan collapsing an
  all-`Z` string to `Z_c` *while fixing `X_c`* costs at most one CNOT per support wire (a
  `Finset.filter` of `Fin n`), the `X_c`-preserving companion of
  `exists_cliffordCircuit_cnotClear_toSingleZ_length_le`.
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reduceSecondFixingX_length_le` (`≤ 4·n`) — the `X_c`-preserving
  `Z`-reduction of the partner image: a diagonalising layer (`≤ 3·n`; per-wire words
  `rotateToZ ≤ 2`, the pivot `rotateToZFixingX ≤ 3`) then the fan above (`≤ n`).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_fixPairSigns_length_le` (`≤ input + 18`) — sign normalisation with at most
  two constant-length `onWire` sign gadgets (`≤ 9` each).
* `exists_cliffordCircuit_reducePair_length_le` (`≤ 6·n + 20`) — the
  first-qubit reduction of an anticommuting pair costs `O(n)` gates, `(2·n + 2) + 4·n + 18`. The
  symplectic first move (`reduceToXGen`, `≤ 2·n + 2`) is reconstructed inline from the
  single-generator bound. This is the per-pair
  `O(n)` atom the first-wire reduction invokes once per induction level.

**The per-level linear bound — the first-wire reduction costs `O(n)` gates**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCountFirstPair.lean`): threads the pair-reduction length bound up
through the first-wire reduction, the per-level bound used in the `O(n²)` unroll.

* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_cliffordCircuit_fixFirstPair_length_le` (`≤ 6·(n+1) + 32`) — the
  length-annotated companion of `exists_cliffordCircuit_fixFirstPair`. For any
  `(n+1)`-qubit normalizer `U`, a Clifford circuit `B` with `B.toMatrix * U` again a normalizer,
  fixing `X₀, Z₀` under conjugation, and `|B| ≤ 6·(n+1) + 32` gates. The `fixFirstPair` lemma hides
  its `B` behind an `∃`, so the companion **reconstructs** `B = G ++ (swapWires c 0 ++ E)` with the
  length-annotated pair reduction (`exists_cliffordCircuit_reducePair_length_le`, `E ≤ 6·(n+1) + 20`
  on `Fin (n+1)`), the wire swap (`length_swapWires_le`, `≤ 3`) and one `onWire` sign gadget
  (`exists_cliffordCircuit_onWire_act_sign_length_le`, `≤ 9`), discharging the two
  conjugation-fixing
  facts with the lemma `normalizer_first_fix` of
  `AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerFirstPair.lean` (it is stated over an arbitrary
  reduction circuit, so the reconstructed `B` fixes `X₀, Z₀` by the same identity). Total
  `9 + 3 + (6·(n+1) + 20) = 6·(n+1) + 32` — the per-level `O(n)` overhead of the Figure 10.9
  recurrence the `O(n²)` headline unrolls.

**The `O(n²)` synthesis + the Theorem 10.6 headline**
(`AxQM/Concrete/PauliNormalizerGateCountSynthesis.lean`): the final assembly, unrolling the
per-level `O(n)` bound through the Figure 10.9 recurrence to the `O(n²)` total, and packaging both
inclusions.

* `isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit_length_le` — **the `O(n²)` gate count (explicit
  polynomial form).** The Figure 10.9 induction (`isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit`) re-run
  with a length-tracking invariant: every `n`-qubit Pauli normalizer `U` equals a global phase times
  a Clifford circuit `U = c • C.toMatrix` (`‖c‖ = 1`) with `C.length ≤ 9·n² + 105·n`. The invariant
  is *tight*: the step `C_{n+1} = inverse B ++ relabel C'` costs `≤ 3·|B| + |C'|` gates
  (`length_inverse_append_relabel_le`) and `|B| ≤ 6·(n+1) + 32`
  (`exists_cliffordCircuit_fixFirstPair_length_le`), so
  `3·(6·(n+1)+32) + (9·n²+105·n) = 9·(n+1)² + 105·(n+1)` reproduces the invariant exactly. The
  matrix content (block-diagonal generator matching + Clifford-circuit inverse) is reproduced
  verbatim from the existence proof.
* `isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit_bigO` — **Theorem 10.6 proper.** A *single universal*
  constant `K` (here `114`) with: for every `n` and every `n`-qubit Pauli normalizer `U`, a Clifford
  circuit `C` and unit phase `c` with `U = c • C.toMatrix` and `C.length ≤ K·n²`. The faithful
  reading of "up to a global phase `U` may be composed from `O(n²)` H/S/CNOT gates" — the constant
  is uniform in `n` (a per-`n` `∃ K` would be vacuous). Immediate from the polynomial bound via
  `9·n² + 105·n ≤ 114·n²`.
* `IsPauliNormalizer.smul` / `isPauliNormalizer_iff_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit` — the qualitative
  characterization packaging **both** inclusions: `N(Gₙ)` is *exactly* the Clifford circuits up to a
  global phase, `IsPauliNormalizer U ↔ ∃ C c, ‖c‖ = 1 ∧ U = c • C.toMatrix`. The forward direction
  is the synthesis (converse); the reverse is the easy direction
  (`cliffordCircuit_isPauliNormalizer`) closed under a unit global phase (`IsPauliNormalizer.smul`:
  `|c|² = 1` cancels in the conjugation and `c • V` stays unitary).
